WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.073
Before modelling, look at the shape of returns.

00:00:03.523 --> 00:00:08.761
Here are South Africa's daily returns, standardized, with a normal curve fitted on top.

00:00:09.211 --> 00:00:10.349
The middle looks fine.

00:00:10.799 --> 00:00:12.263
The trouble is in the tails.

00:00:12.713 --> 00:00:18.109
A normal distribution says a move beyond four standard deviations should almost never happen.

00:00:18.559 --> 00:00:21.799
In this data it happens about once every two hundred days.

00:00:22.249 --> 00:00:24.404
One way to measure this is kurtosis.

00:00:24.854 --> 00:00:27.451
A normal distribution has kurtosis three.

00:00:27.901 --> 00:00:31.870
South Africa sits near seven, the stocks between nine and fifteen.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.576
And Tunisia shows over five hundred.

00:00:35.026 --> 00:00:38.517
That number comes from two days in September 2004.

00:00:38.967 --> 00:00:43.445
The index falls by twenty-seven percent, and climbs back the next day.

00:00:43.895 --> 00:00:48.625
That pattern looks like a data error, and the project flags it rather than deleting it.

00:00:49.075 --> 00:00:52.794
Without those two days, Tunisia's kurtosis is about fifteen.

00:00:53.244 --> 00:00:54.980
Either way the lesson is the same.

00:00:55.430 --> 00:01:00.046
A Gaussian model treats extreme days as near impossible, so it underweights them.

00:01:00.496 --> 00:01:03.532
We will come back to that with heavier-tailed distributions.
